  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nIt's another complex and challenging forecast tonight as quasi-zonal \nflow continues across the middle of the country, with stronger jet \nenergy to our north and low-level jet fed storms across eastern \nKansas this morning. Through the remainder of the overnight into \nSunday morning, low-level flow will increase further north across \nnortheastern Kansas and northwestern Missouri. This is resulting in \nrenewed convective development from north central Kansas into \nnortheastern Kansas. As this low-level jet, and convergence on its \nnose, shift east this morning, we should see showers and storms \ndrift east as well, moving into northwestern Missouri as result. \nWith the waning of the low-level jet during the day, precipitation \nshould decrease with time. Tonight however, as the low-level jet \nreinvigorates, strong moisture transport will lead to anomalously \nhigh precipitable water values streaming into eastern Kansas and \nwestern Missouri. We should see new convective development anywhere \nfrom eastern Kansas to western Missouri, most likely in the 06Z to \n09Z time frame, on the nose of the low-level jet. This corresponds \nto very strong isentropic ascent from the 305K surface through the \n310K surface. Given all these trends, have decreased PoPs through \nthe day, then ramp them up again tonight into tomorrow morning. While \nthe overall threat of severe weather looks low today and tonight, \ncan't rule out a strong storm capable of some strong winds and\nhail, especially tonight into tomorrow morning.  \n\nAdditional rounds of showers and storms are likely through at least \nthe middle part of the week. The setup for Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning looks particularly interesting with regards to \nheavy rainfall. A frontal boundary is likely to stall in the area \nwith deep flow generally parallel to that boundary. Copious amounts \nof moisture are transported north into the area from a strong low-\nlevel jet. This leads to precipitable water values above the 99th \npercentile, pushing 2 to 2.25\". With deep flow parallel to the \nboundary, training storms are likely, and with the anomalously moist \nairmass in place, heavy rainfall and potential for flash flooding is \nbecoming more likely. A large slight risk for excessive rainfall has \nbeen placed over much of the forecast area. It's possible, as \nconfidence in placement and magnitude increase, this will be \nupgraded. We may need a flood watch at some point in the next\nday or so to account for the increasing risk of flooding\nrainfall. Cumulatively, the forecast continues to show\nwidespread 3-5\" rain amounts for northern Missouri, mainly north\nof the Missouri River. \n\nWith regards to the potential for heat, the southern portions of the \nforecast may see highs in the low to mid 90s Monday and Tuesday, \nwith dewpoints climbing into the low to mid 70s. This leads to heat \nindex values in the 100 to 105 range. It's possible a heat advisory \nwill be needed for a portion of the forecast area Monday and \nTuesday. But given the potential for storms and cloud cover, \nconfidence is still somewhat low at this point.",
